WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.180
PRESENTER: I am
pleased to welcome

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:17.600
to the platform the
Honorable Walter L. Sullivan,

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:23.050
Mayor of the city of
Cambridge, and Mr. William R.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:25.660
Hewlett, Vice Chairman of
the board of Hewlett-Packard

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:26.950
company.

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.460
Mr. Hewlett will now give
the commencement address.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:42.798 --> 00:00:45.300
WILLIAM R. HEWLETT: I see I
brought my California weather

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:45.800
with me.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.830
I apologize.

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:52.990
Graduating class of
1986, relatives, faculty,

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:55.990
and friends, it's a
very great pleasure

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:58.690
to be with you this
morning, because it's

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:03.564
exactly 50 years since I
walked across the stage at MIT

00:01:03.564 --> 00:01:05.230
and I was given of a
piece of paper that

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.620
said I was a Master of Science.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.260
In those 50 years, I have
been intimately associated

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.210
with the creative
process that is

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:17.420
so important to our modern
high technology, culture.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:19.900
I felt that it might be
appropriate, therefore,

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.500
to share with you some
random thoughts on creativity

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.040
and its importance
in today's society.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.370
When I was preparing
this address,

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.400
I happened to ask Chuck House,
who heads our engineering

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.480
productivity department what
he thought about creativity.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.880
With a twinkle in
his eye, Chuck said,

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.164
creativity is what screws
up my engineering program.

00:01:45.164 --> 00:01:46.080
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.930
Unfortunately, there is much
truth in that statement.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:52.830
Thomas Edison is alleged to have
remarked about his laboratory,

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.780
there ain't no
rules around here,

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.610
we're trying to
accomplish something.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:00.240
I cite these two comments,
because they say a great deal

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.730
about the creative process.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.670
It works best when it
is not too structured,

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:10.169
but it must, in the long run,
be tamed, harnessed, and hitched

00:02:10.169 --> 00:02:13.490
to the wagon of man's needs.

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:15.500
First, let me set a
background to this country's

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:19.460
renewed interest in
innovation and creativity.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:23.030
By 1983, it was
increasingly evident

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.880
that we were losing the
competitive edge that

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:30.050
for so long had characterized
the American economy.

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:32.000
To better understand
this problem,

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.050
President Reagan created
a high level commission

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:37.640
of distinguished business
leaders and educators

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:42.230
to review means of increasing
the long term competitiveness

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:45.620
of the United States
industry at home and abroad

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:49.580
with particular emphasis
on high technology.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:51.770
After an in-depth
study, the commission

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.560
concluded they were not meeting
the competitive challenge

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:55.820
well enough.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:59.450
It stated, our ability to
compete in world markets

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:00.980
is eroding.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:04.300
Growth and US productivity
lags far behind that

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.230
of our foreign competitors.

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:09.140
Real hourly compensation
for our workforce

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:11.000
is no longer improving.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.240
US leadership and world
trade is declining.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.050
Finally, free tax rate of
return on assets invested

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:20.630
in manufacturing
discourages investment

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:24.770
in this vital core
of our economy.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.440
One of the commission's
primary recommendations

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.730
was to create, apply,
and protect technology.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:36.320
Innovation spurs new industries
and revise mature ones.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.660
Technological advances lead
to improved productivity,

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.800
an essential ingredient
of our standard of living.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.130
In essence, this
recommendation was twofold--

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:50.700
to create technology and
to improve productivity.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.120
How do I define creativity?

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.000
Nobel Prize winner
Szent-Gyorgyi provided

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.280
a good working
definition when he said,

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.790
discovery consists of looking at
the same thing as everyone else

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:03.881
and thinking something
quite different.

00:04:03.881 --> 00:04:05.630
That's a good enough
summary to cover much

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:08.300
of what I wish to comment on.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.990
The trouble is that
creativity is really

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:14.300
a poly-faceted discipline.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:17.000
Trying to describe
it in detail reminds

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.459
me of the story of the
three blind men who

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:21.829
attempted to describe
an elephant solely

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:24.080
by their sense of feel.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.270
One explored the
trunk, one discovered

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:28.140
the tusks, the
third investigated

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:30.050
the animal's enormous feet.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.390
But none really
had a good picture

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:36.380
of what the whole
animal looked like.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:39.090
I would like to describe
my elephant, creativity,

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:43.520
as I've had a chance to
observe it over these years.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.780
First, let me make one general
comment about creative people.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:51.802
Education is not a [INAUDIBLE]
known for being creative.

00:04:51.802 --> 00:04:55.640
A case at point is that of the
most successful engineer who

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:57.710
worked for us for many years.

00:04:57.710 --> 00:04:59.210
Despite the fact
that he had only

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.680
completed one year
of college, he

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.370
was one of the most creative
people I have known.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:07.220
You could present Larry with
the most difficult problem,

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:10.520
and he would come up with
really ingenious solutions.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.910
He had the ability to isolate
the essentials of the problem

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:15.830
and tackle it with vigor.

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:18.770
It was such a pleasure to
talk to him about his work.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:21.350
He just bubbled over
with enthusiasm.

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:24.350
Many of his ideas are still
incorporated in products

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.860
we are currently manufacturing.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:30.260
It is very difficult to
spot a creative individual

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:32.690
just by looking at a resume.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:34.460
Psychologists can't
even agree on how

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.320
to measure this characteristic,
let alone predict

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:39.200
who will display it.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.950
Establishing an environment that
fosters creativity and observe

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:45.470
those who flourish is
probably the best way

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.770
of finding this elusive talent.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:51.340
I would like to distinguish
between two different kinds

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.060
of creativity.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:55.690
One is spontaneous in
which an individual

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:58.090
sees a complete and
elegant solution

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:00.370
to an interesting problem.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:03.060
The other I call
creativity on demand

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:07.260
in which specific objectives are
established that must be met,

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:09.160
both a great deal of
flexibility and how

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:12.640
the results are to be achieved.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:15.430
Both types of
successful innovators

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:17.890
share many traits in common.

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:21.310
Creative people have an abiding
curiosity and an insatiable

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:25.010
desire to learn how
and why things work.

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:26.590
They take nothing for granted.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:28.480
They are interested
in things around them

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:31.450
and tend to stow away bits
and pieces of information

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:33.840
in their mind for future use.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.550
And they have a great ability
to mobilize their thinking

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:40.444
and experiences for use
in solving a new problem.

00:06:40.444 --> 00:06:42.250
Problems, however,
are rarely solved

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:43.780
on the spur of the moment.

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:46.180
They must be organized
and dissected,

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:49.150
then key issues
isolated and defined.

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:51.940
A period of [INAUDIBLE]
then sets in, during which

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:53.880
these issues are mulled over.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.010
You put them in your mind, and
consciously or unconsciously,

00:06:57.010 --> 00:07:01.960
you work at them at odd hours
of day or night, even at work.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:03.790
It is somewhat
analogous to trying

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:07.840
to place a name on the face of
someone that you've met before.

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:09.760
Often, the solution
to the problem

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:12.280
comes to you much the same
way that you eventually

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:14.790
recall the name.

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:17.110
There is a subclass of
individuals that not only have

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:19.720
the ability to see
things around them,

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:22.660
but also to note that which
differs from the norm.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:24.310
A good example is
Sir Arthur Fleming,

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:26.080
who discovered penicillin.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:28.630
His story is familiar
to many of you.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:31.930
He noted that when a mold
had landed on a culture disk

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:35.170
with a colony of
Staphylococci, the colonies

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:37.640
adjacent to the
mold were killed.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:40.930
Many other scientists may
have had the same problem,

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:42.790
but it simply passed by.

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:44.650
In fact, Fleming
himself remembered

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:46.170
such a previous occurrence.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:50.878
But this time, he
became more interested

00:07:50.878 --> 00:07:54.260
and concluded the mold must
have secreted something

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:57.950
that was toxic to the bacteria,
and if so that something might

00:07:57.950 --> 00:08:01.280
be beneficial in the
treatment of human infection.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:04.070
Thus, the drug
penicillin was born.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:06.530
I have observed the
same creative phenomena

00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:11.030
and the same questioning
minds a number of times.

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:14.840
A few years ago, a young British
engineer who was working for us

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:16.940
was investigating
the characteristics

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:19.250
of a new semiconductor device.

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:22.970
In all the tests, it performed
basically as expected.

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:24.380
But Frank noted
that there seemed

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:27.560
to be an excess of noise
in his measurement.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.680
He completed his project
but was still curious.

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:34.070
He decided to look into the
source of his excess noise.

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:36.919
With the aid of a newly
developed measuring technique,

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:40.159
he discovered that the noise
was, in fact, a very high order

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:44.960
harmonic train, essentially an
electrical analogy of a water

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:46.050
hammer.

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:49.220
This proved to be a
very important discovery

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:51.680
for certain nanosecond
measurements.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:54.492
In fact, it made practical
the very technology

00:08:54.492 --> 00:08:55.700
that was used to identify it.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.790
Intellectual curiosity is a
great source of creativity.

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:08.420
An example is that demonstrated
by Nobel Prize winner Luis

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:12.100
Alvarez, a longtime
member of our board.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:15.170
While visiting Egypt, Luis
became interested in the fact

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:17.510
that no major
burial chambers have

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:19.790
been found in the
periods of Khefren

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:23.330
and began to muse about
how one might determine

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:25.560
if such a chamber did exist.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:27.650
Luis was familiar
with cosmic rays

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:30.050
and knew they might be suitable
for X-raying the pyramid.

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:44.930
Careful investigation
led him to the conclusion

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:47.330
that, if he had been asked
to design a proper radiation

00:09:47.330 --> 00:09:49.520
source for this
purpose, it would

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:52.370
have been the mu meson,
an important component

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:56.090
of cosmic rays that
exactly fitted the need.

00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:58.880
An added bonus was the
fact that under the pyramid

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:00.710
was a chamber that
extended the full length

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:03.680
of the base, in which he
could locate his film--

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:06.050
that is, a series
of detectors that

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:08.810
could measure the
strength of the mu meson

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:11.270
as well as the
direction of arrival.

00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:13.160
With these thoughts
in mind, he went

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:15.560
to the chancellor
of US Berkeley, who

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:17.960
on the basis of Luis'
analysis provided

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:21.140
a small amount of money to
carry out the experiment.

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:23.945
I mention this, because it
is a good example of how

00:10:23.945 --> 00:10:27.600
a little support can
encourage a creative person.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:29.330
The study was not
a normal function

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:33.080
of a physics department, nor
was financing of it easy.

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:35.000
It was just a
fascinating subject

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:39.150
the chancellor thought might add
to man's knowledge of the past.

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:41.840
Luis received the
enthusiastic support

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:44.720
of the Egyptian government, and
the experiment in due course

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:46.190
took place.

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:47.550
No chamber was discovered.

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:49.730
Yet the experiment
was successful,

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:52.580
because Luis was able to
prove beyond doubt that there

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:56.270
was no major chamber
in the pyramid.

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:59.210
Another example is
that of an HP scientist

00:10:59.210 --> 00:11:02.660
who was a specialist in
designing quartz crystals used

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:05.870
for generating extremely
accurate time standards.

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:08.360
The accuracy of such
reference crystals

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:10.660
is very sensitive to
change in temperature

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:12.560
and mechanical force.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:14.360
Much of the existing
technology used

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:16.670
to stabilize these
crystals has to do

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:20.030
with minimizing the effect
of these two variables.

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:21.530
Don took the opposite view.

00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:24.380
He thought, why not use
the intrinsic sensitivity

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:28.340
of temperature as a way to
produce a digital thermometer?

00:11:28.340 --> 00:11:30.020
Normally, the
frequency change is not

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:32.210
a linear function
of temperature,

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:35.660
but Don devised a technique
to linearize this relationship

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:38.930
to about a tenth of a degree
over a range from minus 40

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:42.060
to 200 degrees centigrade and
with the resolution of about

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:44.630
a ten thousandth of a degree.

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:46.970
A major advantage
of this technique,

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:49.049
other than that
that are obvious,

00:11:49.049 --> 00:11:50.465
was that the sensing
element could

00:11:50.465 --> 00:11:54.530
be located as far as 10,000 feet
from the measuring instrument

00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:56.870
with no loss of accuracy.

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:59.870
In a similar fashion, he
produced a quartz transducer

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:03.410
that operated at pressures up
to 10,000 pounds per square inch

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:05.180
with an accuracy of
about a twentieth

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:07.760
of a pound per square inch
and a resolution of about

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:10.250
a thousandth of a BSI.

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:12.260
This device has been
used extensively

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:15.050
in oceanographic studies and
for measuring differential

00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:17.540
pressures in oil fields
to determine the best

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:20.240
pumping strategy.

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:23.180
Basically, I've been talking
about creativity applied

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:26.030
to research type situations.

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:27.770
Now, let me turn
to the development

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:34.790
phase, where creativity on
demand is the usual mode.

00:12:34.790 --> 00:12:37.970
Here, creativity's role
is slightly different.

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:40.320
There is now a clearly
defined objective,

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:43.590
and the job is to find
a way to meet this goal.

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:46.040
This is the type of
creativity about which

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:49.880
much has been written, primarily
because techniques to stimulate

00:12:49.880 --> 00:12:52.160
and enhance it are well known.

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:54.830
One book on this subject
I greatly enjoyed

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:58.790
was Conceptual Blockbusting
by James L. Adams.

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:00.680
His title suggests
that we all suffer

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:03.950
from mental blocks that stand
in the way of solving problems

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:05.220
at hand.

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:08.360
These might be emotional
blocks, such as fear

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:13.340
of failure, frustration, or too
much or too little motivation.

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:16.070
They may be perceptual
blocks, such as using

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:18.770
incorrect information
or the wrong method

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:20.630
or not all your senses.

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:23.540
Quite often, they are cultural
blocks, which sometimes

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:25.980
can be the hardest to overcome.

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:28.160
I think this is an area in
which younger people have

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:31.340
a tremendous advantage,
since, as you well know,

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:34.400
they have an enduring habit of
always questioning past wisdom

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:35.810
and authority.

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:38.840
They say to themselves,
there must be a better way.

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:41.520
99 times out of
100, they discover

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:44.270
that existing traditional
ways are the best.

00:13:44.270 --> 00:13:46.610
But it is that 1% that counts.

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:49.190
This is how progress is made.

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:50.960
A wonderful example
of the desire

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:54.020
to break from the rigid
traditions of the past

00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:56.940
may be that of the French
impressionist painters

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:59.760
during the latter part
of the 19th century.

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:01.890
The most important works
of these painters had been

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:05.630
excluded from the Salon, held
in Paris nearly every year under

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:08.690
the auspices of Les
Académie des Beaux-Arts.

00:14:08.690 --> 00:14:11.870
The Salon juries
refused their work,

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:14.330
because they did not
follow the traditions

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:16.410
of the academic schools.

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:20.300
Finally in 1874, the
impressionists, several of them

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:22.760
in their 20s and
30s, banded together

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:26.470
to share the cost of exhibiting
their own controversial works.

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:29.150
And a series of eight of
these impressionistic exhibits

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:30.130
were held.

00:14:30.130 --> 00:14:33.140
And by 1886, the
impressionist school

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:35.480
was well established
and recognized.

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:39.620
But now to return to the
more mundane world of R&amp;D.

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:44.130
Projects do not always progress
at a steady uniform rate.

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:48.440
Sometimes, progress is stymied
by very difficult problems.

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:52.820
The problem might be overcome
by clear technical breakthrough.

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:55.190
But more often than
not, it is bypassed

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:58.850
with a compromised solution
I like to call a hot patch.

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:01.040
If there are too many
of these hot patches,

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:04.400
you probably will wind up
with a very clumsy solution

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:05.960
to the problem.

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:09.530
My company has often employed
retired engineers or scientists

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:11.690
on a part time consulting base.

00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:15.740
One such was Harald Friis of
Bell Telephone Laboratories.

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:17.750
I am sure that some of
the electrical engineers

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:20.390
in the audience are familiar
with his book on antenna

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:21.827
design.

00:15:21.827 --> 00:15:24.410
Harald and his wife would come
to California during the winter

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:29.180
months and spend time in and
around Palo Alto Harald enjoyed

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:31.910
visiting with our engineers
in the laboratories.

00:15:31.910 --> 00:15:34.220
Frankly, he didn't know
beans about modern transistor

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:36.890
circuitry, but he knew how to
think about the development

00:15:36.890 --> 00:15:39.590
process, and he loved to
work with the engineers who

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:43.160
felt they had reached a
dead end on a project.

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:47.060
He would get them to step back
and view their work as a whole.

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:50.060
He would ask, what are
you really trying to do?

00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:52.040
Are you on the right
track, or do you

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:54.260
feel you have too
many hot patches?

00:15:54.260 --> 00:15:56.390
Or are you really
on the wrong track

00:15:56.390 --> 00:15:58.730
and need to make a fresh start?

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:01.520
This can be one of the toughest
decisions a development

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:04.040
engineer may have to make.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:07.110
Harald was just wonderful in
helping young engineers reach

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:08.840
this critical decision.

00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:11.940
He had a way of making a person
see things in perspective,

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:14.490
and engineers just loved him.

00:16:14.490 --> 00:16:16.670
We always try to encourage
this kind of interaction

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:19.580
in our laboratories
between generations,

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:21.230
because experienced
engineers can

00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:24.220
be very helpful in the
development process.

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:26.780
In this exchange of
ideas, I have often

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:29.810
seen younger researchers
learn new ways to harness

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:32.870
the use of their instincts.

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:35.960
In practice,
spontaneous creativity

00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:39.560
and normal development process
are often intertwined and may,

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:41.590
in fact, seem to conflict.

00:16:41.590 --> 00:16:43.550
Witness Chuck House's
comments referred

00:16:43.550 --> 00:16:47.880
to earlier, there is a time
and a place for creativity,

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:50.750
but in the development
process timing sometimes

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:52.760
outweighs innovation.

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:54.820
It all comes back
to the question

00:16:54.820 --> 00:16:57.980
of how often you can change
course and still make forward

00:16:57.980 --> 00:16:59.630
progress.

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:01.490
I remember when we were
trying to bring out

00:17:01.490 --> 00:17:04.400
our first scientific
desktop calculator.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:06.940
Integrated circuits were
just being introduced.

00:17:06.940 --> 00:17:10.579
And we had to decide whether to
delay the entry of the project,

00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:13.130
so that we might use
integrated circuits,

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:16.339
or go ahead and introduce it
with a primitive but proven

00:17:16.339 --> 00:17:18.200
read-only memory device.

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:19.780
We chose the latter.

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:24.920
Timing was a dominant factor and
not the nicest to the solution.

00:17:24.920 --> 00:17:29.130
I have talked enough about
creativity in the R&amp;D phase.

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:30.560
Now, let's look
at how creativity

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:34.400
can help increase productivity
and improve quality.

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:37.730
This challenge is clearly
defined by the findings

00:17:37.730 --> 00:17:39.710
of the President's commission.

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:43.070
We need the same creative
effort in the production process

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:45.860
that we now lavish on
the development phase.

00:17:45.860 --> 00:17:49.280
We must start by having
productivity and quality

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:51.860
as stated objectives of the
research and development

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:53.060
program.

00:17:53.060 --> 00:17:55.670
Productivity must be
designed under a project,

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:57.650
not added at a later date.

00:17:57.650 --> 00:18:00.740
Quality cannot be inspected in.

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:04.430
There was already a great deal
of technology readily available

00:18:04.430 --> 00:18:07.460
that can be used to improve
quality and manufacturability

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:09.200
of a product.

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:11.750
In many cases, however,
the US industries

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:15.110
have not taken advantage of this
knowledge, although much of it

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:17.540
originated in the United States.

00:18:17.540 --> 00:18:20.910
We need new ideas and new
leadership in this quest.

00:18:20.910 --> 00:18:24.399
Here, the universities have
a very real responsibility.

00:18:24.399 --> 00:18:25.940
I don't mean that
they should go back

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:29.090
to teaching forge, foundry,
and machine shop, subjects

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:32.540
that I had to take as
an engineering student.

00:18:32.540 --> 00:18:34.280
But our universities
do need to provide

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:37.940
a theoretical base for
quality and efficiency

00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:40.040
in the manufacturing process.

00:18:40.040 --> 00:18:42.620
I am delighted to learn
that this trend is already

00:18:42.620 --> 00:18:44.630
underway at this institution.

00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:47.360
But after all, what
else would you expect.

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:49.700
I hope I've made it
clear that creativity

00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:52.820
will play a vital and critical
role in our increasingly

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:54.770
high tech society.

00:18:54.770 --> 00:18:57.770
Our company president, John
Young, put it this way.

00:18:57.770 --> 00:19:02.000
Creativity is the only American
competitive advantage left.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:05.674
Industry is going to have to
make some dramatic changes

00:19:05.674 --> 00:19:08.090
in how it views the importance
of research and development

00:19:08.090 --> 00:19:12.080
programs and the necessity
of increasing productivity.

00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:15.420
Change never comes easy.

00:19:15.420 --> 00:19:17.570
There's comfort in
safety and tradition.

00:19:17.570 --> 00:19:20.030
But change must come
no matter how painful

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:22.940
or how expensive it may be.

00:19:22.940 --> 00:19:24.770
From your standpoint,
this situation

00:19:24.770 --> 00:19:26.450
is anything but bleak.

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:28.820
Personal advancement
in a static company

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:32.720
or a static industry can be slow
and difficult. Change, however,

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:36.020
opens cracks in those
monolithic structures.

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:38.190
It presents a period
of great opportunity,

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:40.500
for this is the time when
the best and the most

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:42.840
creative minds we
sought out and placed

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:45.570
in positions of responsibility.

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:47.770
In the high tech
field, top leadership

00:19:47.770 --> 00:19:50.940
was always looking for good
minds, high energy levels,

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:53.530
and willingness to
accept responsibility.

00:19:53.530 --> 00:19:56.222
In fact, our company is so
dependent upon creativity

00:19:56.222 --> 00:19:58.680
that we're still emphasizing
the recruitment of engineering

00:19:58.680 --> 00:20:02.310
graduates, even though there
is a freezing hire in effect

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:04.200
for the rest of the company.

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:07.260
It may turn out that the present
period will be looked back

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:10.360
upon as one of
unprecedented opportunity

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:12.450
for a scientifically minded.

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:14.850
You are in a unique
position to take

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:16.950
advantage of this situation.

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:17.910
Good luck.

00:20:17.910 --> 00:20:21.600
I wish you success, happiness,
and great bursts of creativity

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:23.340
in all of your endeavors.

00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:26.390
[APPLAUSE]

